1
00:00:02,607 --> 00:00:05,081
Today, two tricks
from the "idle" family:

2
00:00:05,314 --> 00:00:08,253
the "crank idle"
and the "seat on side idle".

3
00:00:08,653 --> 00:00:23,321
[Music]

4
00:00:23,374 --> 00:00:25,394
As for the position
and how an "idle" works,

5
00:00:25,460 --> 00:00:27,794
check out my "idle" video.

6
00:00:27,833 --> 00:00:30,654
It's exactly the same:
the wheel goes in front, then behind,

7
00:00:30,913 --> 00:00:33,308
and the wheel makes
a half-turn each time.

8
00:00:33,607 --> 00:00:37,341
To start, the easiest position
is one foot on the crank

9
00:00:37,713 --> 00:00:39,493
and the other foot on the pedal.

10
00:00:42,894 --> 00:00:44,855
Since your first foot is on the crank,

11
00:00:45,094 --> 00:00:48,794
there's often not enough room
to put the other one fully on the pedal.

12
00:00:48,840 --> 00:00:51,660
You end up on just your toes,
but that's fine, it works really well.

13
00:00:51,726 --> 00:00:59,741
[Music]

14
00:00:59,894 --> 00:01:03,421
As with every "idle",
the wheel goes half a turn back

15
00:01:03,488 --> 00:01:04,520
and half a turn forward.

16
00:01:04,587 --> 00:01:06,381
To keep your body over the hub,

17
00:01:06,467 --> 00:01:08,640
keep the unicycle slightly tilted.

18
00:01:08,713 --> 00:01:15,391
[Music]

19
00:01:15,497 --> 00:01:16,825
If you keep it too straight,

20
00:01:17,284 --> 00:01:20,398
it won't work.
You'll always end up off to the side.

21
00:01:20,791 --> 00:01:22,417
Since the saddle is wedged
against your hip,

22
00:01:22,543 --> 00:01:26,731
there's no need at all to hold it
or press on it with your hand.

23
00:01:27,031 --> 00:01:29,858
To start, same rules as always:

24
00:01:29,904 --> 00:01:32,930
hold on to something
and practice, practice, practice.

25
00:01:32,996 --> 00:01:39,381
[Music]

26
00:01:39,661 --> 00:01:41,734
For the "seat on side idle",

27
00:01:42,104 --> 00:01:45,481
the rules and the unicycle position
are exactly the same.

28
00:01:45,681 --> 00:01:49,620
The unicycle is also tilted a little
so you can stay over the wheel.

29
00:01:54,044 --> 00:01:56,326
Practicing "idles" can get boring.

30
00:01:56,350 --> 00:01:58,031
Hold on to a wall,

31
00:01:58,071 --> 00:02:01,091
do it over and over,
chat with someone,

32
00:02:01,171 --> 00:02:04,950
or do at least five minutes of it
at the start of every session.

33
00:02:07,024 --> 00:02:09,631
Getting back on the pedals
from a "crank idle" isn't very hard.

34
00:02:09,844 --> 00:02:15,577
Just lift the leg on the crank side
and put the saddle back under your butt.

35
00:02:15,656 --> 00:02:23,661
[Music]

36
00:02:23,727 --> 00:02:28,757
Getting into a "crank idle" is harder
from regular, seated riding.

37
00:02:29,928 --> 00:02:31,460
It takes a bit of practice.

38
00:02:36,121 --> 00:02:38,481
When the foot still on the pedal
reaches the bottom,

39
00:02:39,174 --> 00:02:43,140
pull the saddle out from behind
and move it to the side.

40
00:02:43,206 --> 00:02:53,998
[Music]

41
00:02:54,464 --> 00:02:56,510
To get into a "seat on side idle",

42
00:02:57,068 --> 00:03:00,137
it's easiest with the saddle already out,
either behind you or to the side.

43
00:03:00,203 --> 00:03:06,738
[Music]

44
00:03:06,804 --> 00:03:07,932
Or, in one movement,

45
00:03:08,003 --> 00:03:10,050
pull it out
and go straight into the "idle".

46
00:03:10,116 --> 00:03:16,228
[Music]

47
00:03:16,327 --> 00:03:18,774
To get back on the saddle,
you have a few options.

48
00:03:18,801 --> 00:03:22,128
Either pass it behind you
if the fork isn't too tall,

49
00:03:22,394 --> 00:03:27,294
or lean on the bottom pedal
and lift the other leg back over,

50
00:03:27,340 --> 00:03:30,340
the same move
you did with the "crank idle".

51
00:03:30,406 --> 00:03:36,888
[Music]

52
00:03:36,954 --> 00:03:39,929
Since the unicycle position
is the same for the "crank idle"

53
00:03:39,953 --> 00:03:41,595
and the "seat on side idle",

54
00:03:41,674 --> 00:03:43,668
it's very easy to switch between the two.

55
00:03:44,087 --> 00:03:49,140
You just move the foot on the pedal
over to the crank, and so on.

56
00:03:49,246 --> 00:04:03,655
[Music]

57
00:04:03,767 --> 00:04:05,403
Once you're comfortable with these,

58
00:04:05,427 --> 00:04:08,207
as usual, you can take them further,

59
00:04:08,233 --> 00:04:11,300
for example, by crabbing sideways
or spinning on the spot.

60
00:04:11,366 --> 00:04:25,165
[Music]

61
00:04:25,231 --> 00:04:28,966
You can also try finding
new ways in and out of the tricks.

62
00:04:28,989 --> 00:04:31,985
Bear in mind, the "crank idle"
may be the first trick you learn

63
00:04:32,009 --> 00:04:35,725
with the opposite foot on the crank,

64
00:04:35,749 --> 00:04:39,210
which leads to all the reversed tricks,
like the "cross ride".

65
00:04:39,276 --> 00:04:50,535
[Music]

66
00:04:50,587 --> 00:04:53,248
After that, it's up to you
to come up with lots of new stuff.

67
00:04:53,314 --> 00:04:54,400
Good luck!

68
00:04:54,466 --> 00:05:14,546
[Music]

